  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cholera Outbreak Responce in the north of Yemen 24/2025.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The United Nations (UN) led Humanitarian Country Team (HCT) in Yemen declared a cholera outbreak in the north of Yemen on March 19, 2024. Similarly, the Deputy of Ministry for Primary Health Care announced the outbreak of Acute Watery Diarrhoea (AWD) in the north during the monthly UN Health Cluster meeting. The transmission of cholera/AWD rate is very high, with the reported AWD cases doubling just in one week (from week 11 to week 12). As of the end of March, 2024, there are 7,324 reported AWD cases affecting all 15 governorates in Yemen. When announcing the cholera/AWD outbreak, the Health Cluster announced Sa&apos;dah, Ibb, Hajjah, and Tai&apos;z as priority areas, with Sa&apos;dah  - accounting for 33% of total cases  - being the epicenter of the outbreak. The humanitarian situation in Sa&apos;dah, Ibb, and Hajjah is urgent as communities there face the protracted effects related to conflict, displacement, extreme poverty, climate -related disasters, economic insecurity, and protection risks. The multitude of challenges they face mean they have inadequate access to safe Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) services and basic medical services, making them more vulnerable to the cholera/AWD outbreak.  Further, Iftar gathering, incoming rainy season, and potential floods will pose high risk of endemicity and re -emergence. The World Health Organization (WHO) estimates there will be more than 50,000 AWD cases in the next six months if the outbreak is not contained.</narrative>
  </description>
